        <h2>How to Build a Mixed Cooler</h2>
        <p>The mistake most people make is buying one case of one thing. By hour three, nobody wants it. Here's the formula we suggest for a day on the water with a crowd:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>A crushable lager as the base</strong> &mdash; roughly half the cooler. Pearl Snap, Hans' Pils, Shiner, or Lone Star.</li>
          <li><strong>One hoppy option</strong> &mdash; a six-pack of Electric Jellyfish or Fire Eagle for the IPA people.</li>
          <li><strong>A seltzer or canned cocktail</strong> &mdash; Ranch Rider or Topo Chico Hard Seltzer for the folks who want something lighter or spirit-based.</li>
          <li><strong>One wildcard</strong> &mdash; a hefeweizen, a Mexican-style lager, or whatever's new on the singles wall. Ask us.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-alcoholic cans</strong> &mdash; a few NA beers or plain Topo Chico for the designated captain. Boat rules and Texas law don't take the day off.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Pack the cooler in layers: ice, cans, ice, cans, ice on top. Cans that go in cold from our cave stay cold far longer than cans that go in warm.</p>

        <h2>Cases, Singles, and Growlers</h2>
        <p>The cave stocks cases and 12-packs of the most popular beers, so you can grab in bulk without waiting for us to break down a pallet. The singles wall in the cooler lets you build your own mixed six-pack, which is the best way to try five Texas breweries without committing to a case of any of them. If you're a growler person, ask at the counter or call <a href="tel:5126144949">(512) 614-4949</a>; availability changes with what we can source, so we'd rather tell you than promise it here.</p>

        <h2>What About the East Austin Store?</h2>
        <p><a href="/east-austin.html">Our East Austin store</a> at MLK and Chicon is a smaller building and doesn't have a walk-in cave. What it has is a tighter, curated beer selection: the Austin core (Live Oak, Austin Beerworks, Pinthouse, Zilker, Lazarus, Hi Sign), a few rotating seasonals, and the seltzers and canned cocktails that move on the east side. If you want breadth and cases by the dozen, Lakeway is the store. If you want a well-edited cold six-pack on the way to a backyard, East Austin has you.</p>
        <p>Both stores are open Monday through Saturday, 10am to 9pm, and closed Sunday (that's Texas law; our <a href="/guides/texas-liquor-laws-explained.html">liquor laws guide</a> explains it). Heading to the lake on a Sunday? Order Saturday. And if you're new to the area, our <a href="/guides/liquor-stores-near-lake-travis.html">guide to liquor stores near Lake Travis</a> covers how to find us from the marinas.</p>

        <h2>Common Questions</h2>
        <h3>Which Monarch store has the bigger beer selection?</h3>
        <p>Lakeway. It's our biggest store, with a walk-in beer cave and a 17-door cooler. East Austin carries a smaller, curated beer selection focused on Austin breweries and seltzers.</p>
        <h3>Is the beer in the cave actually cold, or just stored there?</h3>
        <p>It's cold. The cave is a refrigerated room kept at fridge temperature around the clock, and beer goes in straight from the distributor's refrigerated truck.</p>
        <h3>Can I get beer and seltzers delivered from the Lakeway store?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Order online for delivery or curbside pickup, Monday through Saturday during store hours. A 21+ adult with valid photo ID must receive the order. See our <a href="/delivery.html">delivery page</a> for terms.</p>
